{{TAGS trench ai}}

Yet another short post from the [trenches](/tags/trench)!

Asking if we should review AI code looks like a clickbait title. It is not.

By now, you've doubtless heard about the problem. AI is generating so much code
that humans don't have the time to review it. In [a popular Ars Technica
article](https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/ai-coding-agents-generate-more-code-but-not-more-software/)
which has been making the rounds, they discuss a
[Jellyfish](https://jellyfish.co/) study and they argue (emphasis mine):

> The introduction of AI coding agents at a firm leads to a 30 percent increase
in total lines of code generated, a 20 percent rise in the number of total
commits, and a 23 percent increase in pull requests on average, the researchers
write. But all **that extra code doesn’t translate directly into improved
software output on the firm level**.

And they later write, "the researchers found a 14 percent increase in the share
of workers performing code reviews after AI agents’ introduction."

In other words, we're outputting more code, getting bogged down in review, but
not producing more value. Also, [the code itself is often
rubbish](https://lucumr.pocoo.org/2026/9/7/astra-why/).

Anyone else, like myself, who is in the trenches is finding this to be
directionally true. So if productivity gains are offset by time in reviews, what
are we to do?

More than one of the teams I work with has expressed this concern and it's a
serious one.

So what are reviews for? Developers think "finding bugs," but in reality, [it's
about knowledge sharing](https://zenodo.org/records/1401198), and, uh,
[knowledge
sharing](https://research.google/pubs/modern-code-review-a-case-study-at-google/),
and, again, [knowledge
sharing](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/rigby2013convergent.pdf),
and I'll throw in a 2015 _pre-AI_ Microsoft position paper arguing that [code
review does not find
bugs](https://microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2015/05/PID3556473.pdf).

And, to be brutally honest, I'm finding that some effective software teams had
been failing to do code reviews _before_ they started to use AI. That gives me a
lot of pause because I've long been a huge advocate of code reviews.

All of this came to a head when a developer asked why [PAAD's
`/agentic-review`](https://github.com/Ovid/paad#the-skills) skill can't be used
in lieu of the code reviews which are already either not being done, or are not
great at finding bugs.[% Ovid.note("Anecdotally, code reviews over a 400-line diff are where
human ability to review breaks down.") %]

Well, I _am_ instituting a new CI/CD process to test out automated code review
via `/agentic-review`, and if there are teams which do little to no code
reviews, we have _something_ which is better, right? Right?

If (and this is a big "if") the main benefit of code review is actually teaching
developers about the codebase, could the steering files (`AGENTS.md`,
`CLAUDE.md`, `.kiro/steering/*.md`, etc.) be kept updated and serve that role?
When you're stuck figuring out what to do with `GET
/legacy/api/v2/customer/{id}`, you often just need a high-level overview of the
code before you dig in. Can the steering files provide that and use the AI to
dig deeper when needed? Is that where we're going? (I think the answer is
"yes, that's where we're going." I don't know whether or not this is where
we _should_ be going.)

Of course, that requires the steering files to be regularly updated and that's
yet another task I'm working with teams on.

I honestly don't have the answer for this. We have a problem and we need to find
solutions. Short of abandoning AI (which some developers might be happy
with), we need creative people thinking _hard_ about this problem. It's not
going to go away, so we need to tackle it head-on.

So I'm working on this now. It will be interesting to see where it goes.

